WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:06.089
Although this may make you want to smack your
head against the wall repeatedly, the fact

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:09.460
of the matter is; everything is maths.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:15.469
From your phone to your house, the tiny particles
that everything is made of and the giant,

00:00:15.469 --> 00:00:22.270
swirling galaxies that dot the infinite universe;
all of that is based on numbers.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:26.000
But before you start throwing tomatoes at
the screen, as you are part of the large majority

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.580
of people who hates numbers and their evil
behavior, don’t worry, no formulas or tests

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:32.580
today.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.130
It’s strange though, isn’t it, this anger
towards something that is so vital to our

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:41.070
lives, and that mathematicians are seen as
strange wizards with their nerdy number magic.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:46.560
So today, we look at magic numbers that rule
your life, and possibly death.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.380
Let’s start with a nice safe one; ten.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:57.610
We all like ten, it’s the girl next door
of mathematics; you get ten, you know ten,

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.100
ten is your friend.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.380
But why is this?

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:04.930
Why are we so comfortable around those lovely
rounded tens, twenties and hundreds but it

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.689
all gets so murky when you veer away from
them?

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:10.310
Well the simple answer is; habit.

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:15.439
The only reason we work in tens is because
we have ten fingers and it just became common

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:17.740
practice to do it like that.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.100
These are called “bases”.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.149
A base is just how you decide to group your
numbers.

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:27.270
We work in base ten so you have the numbers
zero to nine, if you add one more to nine

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.819
then you complete a group of ten, and so you
move onto the next group.

00:01:30.819 --> 00:01:35.850
So if we worked in base four, you’d have
the numbers zero to three and when you add

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.399
one more then you complete a group of four.

00:01:38.399 --> 00:01:44.070
We feel like 10 is logical but an alien society
is just as likely to work in some other base.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:48.039
It wouldn’t be a problem though, we could
easily “translate” it into friendly looking

00:01:48.039 --> 00:01:49.359
tens.

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:54.229
Many cultures have used other bases, either
partially or completely.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:57.420
French for example, mixes in base 20 with
base 10.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.810
“Quatre vingt dix neuf” is 99 but translates
as “four twenties and nineteen”.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:10.709
The Oksapmin people of New Guinea have a base-27
counting system, with each number being named

00:02:10.709 --> 00:02:14.190
after a separate body part, which is how they
keep track.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:20.379
So, I’ll have ear apples, elbow carrots
and belly button slices of bacon please.

00:02:20.379 --> 00:02:24.689
Probably the most important number of all
time is zero.

00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:28.989
It seems like such an obvious thing to us
but it was missing from many early counting

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:29.989
systems.

00:02:29.989 --> 00:02:34.489
The Sumerians were the first people to develop
a counting system but it was all about keeping

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:39.299
stock of cattle and horses so you didn’t
need a number for something that you didn’t

00:02:39.299 --> 00:02:40.299
have.

00:02:40.299 --> 00:02:45.640
It was Brahmagupta, an Indian mathematician
from the 7th century who formalized zero by

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.750
representing it as a dot under a number.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.440
He also wrote rules about its use.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.370
It reached the west through trade from Arabia,
with the Arabian mathematicians making great

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.959
advances in many areas of mathematics and
Mohammed ibn-Musa al-Khowarizmi creating equations

00:02:58.959 --> 00:02:59.959
which resulted in zero, or algebra, as it
is now known.

00:02:59.959 --> 00:03:05.930
The word zero itself is from Arabic, along
with algebra, algorithm and average.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:11.640
Without zero, we never would have been able
to create binary code, which gave us computers.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.189
So life would be very different today; I would
probably be shouting all these facts in some

00:03:16.189 --> 00:03:20.209
town square somewhere and then maybe burnt
as a witch.

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:22.780
Can you tell me what these numbers have in
common?

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:28.609
2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19?

00:03:28.609 --> 00:03:31.459
That’s right, they are prime numbers.

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:36.810
A prime is a number that only divides by one
and by itself, we don’t include 1 as a prime.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.370
Now, for non-mathematical people, you might
think?

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:40.370
Who cares?

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.010
Why does everyone talk about primes like they
are so important?

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:47.959
The thing is, prime numbers are like elements
in chemistry; they are the building blocks

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:50.730
that everything is made of.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:55.970
2,3,5… these are just like Hydrogen, Helium,
Lithium and so on.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:04:01.290
Every non-prime, or composite number as they
are called, can be made by multiplying two

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.659
prime numbers together.

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:05.959
Fifteen, for example is 3 x 5.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:08.280
Ninety-Four is 2 x 47.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:13.319
What’s more, all even numbers are the sum
of two prime numbers.

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:18.660
Ninety Four is 89 + 5
The multiplication of primes may just sound

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:25.380
like a dull quirk of mathematics but it has
a very real benefit for us internet users;

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.730
security.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:32.810
Multiplying two primes together might be easy
but working backwards is very very difficult.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:37.590
If I give you a number and tell you to work
out what primes it’s made up of, you basically

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:42.890
have to try out all the combinations until
you find the correct one; there is no formula.

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:47.420
If it’s a small number, that’s not so
bad but when you get up into something that’s

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.560
hundreds or thousands of digits long, yeah…
good luck with that.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:57.260
So internet security, which is active when
you see HTTPS at the beginning of a web address,

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:03.060
uses this quirk of mathematics to encode your
sensitive data; you need the correct key in

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.320
order to solve this prime multiplication problem
each time.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:11.370
Finding new prime numbers can be a rewarding
business since there are a range of prizes

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:16.840
on offer, with hundreds of thousands of dollars
being offered for ones over 100 million digits

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:17.840
long.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.960
The current record holder for the world’s
largest prime number was only just discovered

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:28.950
in January 2016 and the number itself is over
22 million digits long.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:32.860
Although we picked 10 because of what our
hands look like, there are some numbers that

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:38.550
are important throughout the universe, such
as 3.14… or Pi.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:45.270
Carl Sagan, the famous astrophysicist, wrote
a novel Contact, which became a big movie.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:49.890
When humans find the number Pi in a radio
signal from the stars, we know it’s a sign

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:55.660
of intelligent life because this number must
be universal to everyone, regardless of how

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.500
many fingers the alien species has.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.890
Pi is actually quite a simple concept.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:06.190
If you take a circle and divide its circumference
by its diameter, then you always get the same

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:10.150
answer because this is just the ratio that
makes a circle.

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:16.110
It’s everywhere; car tyres, coke cans, actual
pies and Karl Pilkington’s impressively

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:17.190
round face.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:21.330
And it’s been invaluable for all of science,
and has led engineering advances for thousands

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:25.350
of years.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:31.800
There are other constant numbers like e, which
is 2.718, that is used for many things but

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.350
famously on working out interest in banking.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:37.490
I’m sure we all have a lot of interest in
banking.

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:42.980
But one constant that is much more exciting
is Phi; the golden ratio.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:47.820
The number is about 1.618 and it’s all about
proportions.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:49.070
Take a rectangle and add the short side to
the long side.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:53.630
If the rectangle is in this perfect golden
ratio then the long side will be 1.618 times

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:55.660
longer than the short side.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:02.300
The reason we’re interested in it is that
is seems to appear in nature all the time,

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:12.150
especially when you apply it to spirals; the
seeds of a sunflower, the swirl of a galaxy,

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.550
pinecones, hurricanes.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:18.130
You can see examples of the golden ratio in
ancient buildings and a huge number of paintings.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:23.160
It’s often been suggested that it is the
number of pure beauty and that a face with

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:29.210
features matched in the golden ratio is going
to be the most universally popular, so make

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:32.640
sure you have a ruler out ready, next time
you’re on tinder.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.060
Even if you are one of the many people who
would rather lick a toilet bowl than work

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:41.360
out a percentage, it’s good to know that
maths is out there, doing its job, even without

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:44.480
your help and appreciation, you ungrateful
little monkey.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:48.840
And of course, even though it’s not on this
list, all of us know which number is really

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.400
the secret answer to the universe, don’t
we?

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:56.730
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more … um…
add the three… divide by seven… that’s

00:07:56.730 --> 00:08:16.470
it, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

